# Comments and TSDoc

Always include thorough inline comments, JSDoc/docstrings for functions, and explanations for complex logic. This applies to **all** project code, not one package or algorithm.

Follow [Google’s technical writing style](https://developers.google.com/tech-writing): active voice, present tense, short sentences. Prefer why over what. Restate the change objective in one sentence before editing.

Every new source file starts with a file header (purpose, not a changelog):

```ts
/**
 * @file CapabilityResolver — apply a tenant override onto a base capability artifact.
 */
```

Document every non-trivial function, method, class, and public type with TSDoc (TypeDoc tags only: `@param`, `@returns`, `@throws`, `@example`, `@file`, `@see`). Imperative summary. `@param` / `@returns` / `@throws` when the signature is not enough. Do not restate the type in prose. Do not TSDoc trivial one-liners.

A file header plus function TSDoc is not enough. Comment the **body** at branches, invariants, failure modes, and non-obvious ordering. Skip narration of obvious assignments (`const n = items.length`).

```ts
/**
 * Load the tenant override pinned to this base version.
 *
 * @param tenant - Enrolled tenant id
 * @returns The override, or `undefined` if the tenant is not enrolled
 */
export async function getOverride(tenant: string): Promise<CapabilityOverride | undefined> {
  const override = await this.readOverrideFile(tenant);
  // Fail closed in the caller: missing file means not enrolled, never "use base as-is".
  return override;
}
```
